39:---
40:
41:## Definition
42:
43:Write $X \approx Y$, and say $X$ and $Y$ are **equinumerous**, when there is a
44:bijection $X \to Y$.
45:
46:An ordinal $\kappa$ ([[def-ordinal]]) is a **cardinal**, equivalently an
47:**initial ordinal**, when
48:
49:$$\text{no } \alpha \in \kappa \text{ satisfies } \alpha \approx \kappa,$$
50:
51:that is, $\kappa$ is not equinumerous with any strictly smaller ordinal.
52:
53:**Cardinality, under the Axiom of Choice.** Assume the Axiom of Choice
54:([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) and let $X$ be a set. Then $X$ carries a well-order
55:([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]), which has an order type $\alpha$
56:([[thm-mostowski-collapse]]) and in particular $\alpha \approx X$. Now
57:$\alpha^{+} = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$ is an ordinal ([[lem-ordinal-basics]],
58:claim (c)) whose elements are ordinals (claim (a)) and which contains $\alpha$,
59:so $C = \{\xi \in \alpha^{+} : \xi \approx X\}$ is a nonempty set of ordinals and
60:has an $\in$-least element $\kappa$ ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]). This $\kappa$ is the
61:**cardinality** of $X$, written $|X|$; it is a cardinal, because
62:$\beta \in \kappa$ with $\beta \approx \kappa \approx X$ would lie in
63:$\alpha^{+}$ and contradict the minimality of $\kappa$.
64:
65:**Well-definedness: $\kappa$ does not depend on the well-order or on $\alpha$.**
66:The recipe above instantiates a well-order of $X$ and an order type $\alpha$ for
67:it, and $X$ will in general carry many well-orders with many different order
68:types, so the value $\kappa$ has to be shown independent of both. It is, because
69:$\kappa$ is in fact the least ordinal equinumerous with $X$ **outright**, a
70:description in which neither the well-order nor $\alpha$ appears. Let $\beta$ be
71:any ordinal with $\beta \approx X$. By trichotomy for ordinals
72:([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]) exactly one of $\beta \in \alpha^{+}$,
73:$\beta = \alpha^{+}$, $\alpha^{+} \in \beta$ holds. In the first case
74:$\beta \in C$, so $\kappa \subseteq \beta$ by minimality of $\kappa$. In the
75:other two cases claim (f) of [[lem-ordinal-basics]] gives
76:$\alpha^{+} \subseteq \beta$, and $\alpha \in \alpha^{+}$ because
77:$\alpha^{+} = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$, so $\alpha \in \beta$ and hence
78:$\alpha \subseteq \beta$ by claim (f) again; and $\alpha \in C$, so
79:$\kappa \subseteq \alpha$ by minimality, whence $\kappa \subseteq \beta$. In every case
80:$\kappa \subseteq \beta$, that is $\kappa \le \beta$. So $\kappa$ is the least
81:element of the collection of **all** ordinals equinumerous with $X$, and any two
82:runs of the recipe, from any two well-orders of $X$, return the same $\kappa$.
83:
84:## Remarks
85:
86:- **What the well-definedness argument does and does not need.** The obligation
87:  is that $|X|$ depend on $X$ alone, and it is discharged in the definition
88:  itself, from two lemmas that are genuine prerequisites of this item and
89:  therefore sit in `deps` rather than in `justified_by`: comparability and
90:  trichotomy of ordinals ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]) and the elementary closure
91:  facts ([[lem-ordinal-basics]]). Neither mentions cardinals, so neither points
92:  forward, and no separate discharging lemma is needed. The bound $\alpha^{+}$ is
93:  a device for turning "the least ordinal equinumerous with $X$" into a
94:  Separation instance over a **set**; the argument above is what shows that the
95:  device does not change the answer.
96:
97:- **The definition is choice-free; the cardinality assignment is not.** Being a
98:  cardinal is a property of an ordinal and needs no axiom beyond ZF. Attaching a
99:  cardinality to an arbitrary *set* is a different matter: a set that carries no
100:  well-order is equinumerous with no ordinal at all, so $|X|$ simply does not
101:  exist for it. Without the Axiom of Choice there is no ordinal-valued notion of
102:  size for arbitrary sets, and what survives is [[thm-hartogs]]: every set $A$
103:  has a smallest ordinal $\aleph(A)$ that does not inject into it.
104:- **Most ordinals are not cardinals.** The successor $\omega^{+} = \omega \cup \{\omega\}$
105:  is equinumerous with $\omega \in \omega^{+}$, by the explicit bijection sending
106:  $\omega$ to $0$ and each natural number $n$ to $\sigma(n)$, which is a
107:  bijection because $\sigma$ is injective and its image is exactly the nonzero
108:  natural numbers. So $\omega^{+}$ is an ordinal and not a cardinal, and the
109:  same shift applies to the successor of any ordinal containing $\omega$.
110:  Cardinals are sparse among ordinals, which is
111:  precisely why the least one equinumerous with a given set is a useful
112:  representative.
113:- **Which ordinals up to and including $\omega$ are cardinals.** Every natural
114:  number is a cardinal, and so is $\omega$. Both facts are counting facts rather
115:  than order facts, and both come from the pigeonhole principle
116:  ([[lem-pigeonhole]]), proved on the countability page. Every natural number is
117:  an ordinal, and so is $\omega$ ([[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]], claim (ii));
118:  and if $\alpha \in n$ with $n$ a natural number then $\alpha$ is itself a
119:  natural number, since $\mathbb{N}$ is a transitive set
120:  ([[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]), with $\alpha < n$ by claim (i) of
121:  [[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]].
122:  So if some $\alpha \in n$ had $\alpha \approx n$, claim 3 of the pigeonhole
123:  principle would force $\alpha = n$ and hence $n \in n$, which claim (b) of
124:  [[lem-ordinal-basics]] forbids; therefore $n$ is a cardinal. And if some
125:  $\alpha \in \omega$ had $\alpha \approx \omega$, then $\alpha$ would be a
126:  natural number equinumerous with $\mathbb{N} = \omega$, which claim 4 of the
127:  pigeonhole principle forbids; therefore $\omega$ is a cardinal. Nothing else on
128:  this page depends on either fact, and the definition above is stated so that it
129:  does not.
130:- **Notation.** The infinite cardinals are traditionally written
131:  $\aleph_0, \aleph_1, \dots$, with $\aleph_0 = \omega$. That last equation can
132:  now be stated outright rather than quoted: it says $\omega$ is the least
133:  ordinal equinumerous with $\omega$, which is precisely the assertion that
134:  $\omega$ is a cardinal, established in the previous remark from claim 4 of
135:  [[lem-pigeonhole]]. Nothing below rests on it. The notation
136:  $\aleph(A)$ for the Hartogs number of $A$ ([[thm-hartogs]]) comes from the
137:  same source and is deliberately close: under the Axiom of Choice
138:  $\aleph(A)$ is the successor cardinal of $|A|$.
139:- **Why initial ordinals rather than equivalence classes.** The natural
140:  definition of "cardinal" as the class of all sets equinumerous with a given
141:  one never yields a set, for the same reason as in [[thm-burali-forti]].
142:  Choosing the least ordinal in the class is von Neumann's fix, and it works
143:  exactly when the class contains an ordinal, which is exactly when the set can
144:  be well ordered.
