41:
42:## Definition
43:
44:Let $W$ be a set. A **well-order** on $W$ is a total order $\le$ on $W$
45:([[def-partial-order]]) with the property that
46:
47:$$\text{every nonempty } S \subseteq W \text{ has a least element, i.e. an } \ell \in S \text{ with } \ell \le s \text{ for all } s \in S.$$
48:
49:The pair $(W, \le)$ is then a **well-ordered set**, and $W$ is **well-ordered**
50:by $\le$.
51:
52:A least element of $S$ is unique when it exists: two of them are below each
53:other, hence equal by antisymmetry ([[def-partial-order]]). We may therefore
54:write $\min S$ for it.
55:
56:**Strict form.** Everything on this page is more convenient in terms of the
57:associated strict order $x < y :\iff (x \le y \text{ and } x \ne y)$
58:([[def-partial-order]]). Spelled out strictly, a well-order on $W$ is a relation
59:$<$ that is
60:
61:- **irreflexive**: $x < x$ holds for no $x \in W$;
62:- **transitive**: $x < y$ and $y < z$ imply $x < z$;
63:- **trichotomous**: for all $x, y \in W$ exactly one of $x < y$, $x = y$,
64:  $y < x$ holds;
65:- and such that every nonempty $S \subseteq W$ has an element $\ell$ with no
66:  $s \in S$ satisfying $s < \ell$.
67:
68:The two presentations determine each other by $x \le y \iff (x < y$ or
69:$x = y)$, and we write $(W, <)$ or $(W, \le)$ as convenient.
70:
71:## Remarks
72:
73:- **Totality is not an extra hypothesis.** If $\le$ is a partial order on $W$ in
74:  which every nonempty subset has a least element, then $\le$ is already total:
75:  apply the hypothesis to the two element subset $\{x, y\}$, whose least element
76:  is below the other. Totality is nevertheless stated, because in the strict
77:  presentation trichotomy has to be written down explicitly.
78:- A well-order is total, so **every subset of a well-ordered set is a chain**
79:  ([[def-chain]]), and $W$ itself is one. Chains are therefore not the
80:  interesting invariant here; the least element property is.
81:- **The model case is $(\mathbb{N}, \le)$**, which is a linear order
82:  ([[thm-nat-linear-order]]) in which every nonempty subset has a least element
83:  ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]). Ordinals, defined later on this page, are
84:  the exact generalisation of that picture.
85:- $(\mathbb{Z}, \le)$ and $(\mathbb{R}, \le)$ are total orders but not
86:  well-orders: $\mathbb{Z}$ has no least element at all, and the bounded set
87:  $\{x \in \mathbb{R} : 0 < x < 1\}$ has none either. Being bounded below does
88:  not help, which is exactly why well-ordering is a strong condition.
89:- The empty set carries exactly one well-order, the empty relation, vacuously.
90:  Every one element set carries exactly one.
91:- A well-order admits no infinite strictly decreasing sequence
92:  $x_0 > x_1 > x_2 > \cdots$, since the set of its terms would have no least
93:  element. That direction is a theorem of ZF and is used freely here. The
94:  **converse**, that a total order with no infinite strictly decreasing sequence
95:  is a well-order, is a different matter: the natural argument takes a nonempty
96:  $S$ with no least element and assembles a decreasing sequence inside it by
97:  choosing each term below the previous one, which is exactly the principle of
98:  **dependent choice** (DC), described in [[def-countable-choice]]. DC is not a
99:  theorem of ZF **unless ZF is inconsistent**; that much is recorded in the
100:  ledger ([[rem-choice-ledger]]), which lists DC among the principles not
101:  provable in ZF. Granted the consistency of ZF, the converse above is likewise
102:  unprovable in ZF, and this is a *separate* statement that the ledger does not
103:  record. The witness for it that the library does record is Cohen's first model
104:  ([[rem-cohen-first-model]]), which contains an infinite set
105:  $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ with no countably infinite subset. Order $A$ by the
106:  order it inherits from $\mathbb{R}$: a strictly decreasing sequence in $A$
107:  would be an injection $\mathbb{N} \to A$, so there is none, while $A$ is not
108:  well ordered, since a well-ordered infinite set is order isomorphic to an
109:  ordinal at least $\omega$ ([[thm-mostowski-collapse]]) and so does have a
110:  countably infinite subset. Both statements are external metamathematical
111:  results, established by forcing and permutation models; they are quoted from
112:  the references below, and neither is proved anywhere in this library, which
113:  contains neither technique. Nothing on this page depends on any of it: the
114:  library takes the least element formulation as the definition and never uses
115:  the descending sequence characterisation, precisely so that no result here
116:  inherits that cost.
